  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How the Parts of a Computer Are Wired Together",
  "hero.sub": "Which path do the CPU, memory, graphics card, drives and USB ports each take?<br>Why were early computers built around one bus everyone shared, and why is today’s machine a web of point-to-point links?",
  "hero.lead": "This essay follows thirty years of change: a set of wires taken in turn (PCI), why that set could not run faster, the years when all traffic met in the northbridge, the “near and far” memory that appeared once the memory controller moved into the CPU, the difference between a CPU link and a chipset link on today’s motherboard, the rings and meshes inside a chip, the several chiplets in one package, and finally I²C, the bus that still shares two wires today. Each chapter starts with a structure diagram, then gives a model you can drag.",
  "hero.note": "<strong>A note first:</strong> this essay was written by AI (Claude). Each model is a scaled-down version of something real: the PCI spec’s own time budget, reflections on a transmission line, max-min fair sharing of link bandwidth, Little’s law, hop counts on an on-chip network, and the pull-up resistor formula from the I²C spec. The text says which numbers the models compute and which come from datasheets or papers. Tuned parameters are marked beside their model, with an account of how the conclusion changes if they are halved or doubled.",

