  "ch1.title": "One set of wires everyone shares",
  "ch1.law": "A bus is a set of wires that every device hangs off: only one can talk at a time, so bandwidth is split among everyone. The more devices, the less each gets, and the longer each waits.",
  "ch1.p1": "Picture an office with one telephone. While few people need it, anyone can call whenever they like. When many do, they queue, and someone has to decide who is next. The <strong>PCI bus</strong>, fixed in 1992, is that telephone: a set of wires runs across the motherboard, and the graphics card, network card and disk controller all hang off it in parallel. The spec gives 32 bits wide at 33 MHz, a peak of <strong>{pciPeak} MB/s</strong> (33 MHz × 4 bytes; the 133 MB/s you often see quoted counts the clock as 33.33 MHz). The older ISA bus was shared too, only slower.",
  "ch1.p2": "Sharing has three consequences. First, <strong>addresses and data use the same wires</strong>: PCI sends an address on AD[31:0] in one clock and data on the clocks that follow, a scheme called <strong>multiplexing</strong>. Second, there must be an <strong>arbiter</strong>: each device that wants to talk pulls its own REQ# line, and the arbiter answers on GNT# to say “your turn”. Third, every transfer carries a fixed overhead: the address goes out, the target has to get ready (the spec’s example takes {initLat} clocks for the “target initial latency”), and only then do four bytes a clock start to flow.",
  "ch1.p3": "The PCI spec works this out itself. Its Table 3-5 lists a typical machine: graphics card 50 MB/s, network card 4, disk 5, ISA bridge 4, and 9 reserved for PCI bridges behind it, {t35need} MB/s in all. Assuming each transfer costs {initLat} clocks of overhead, and counting how many transfers each device makes and how many clocks each one takes, the spec finds how long the bus is busy in every {sliceUs} µs: <strong>{t35used} µs</strong>. The model below uses the same rule, and it recomputes the table row by row.",
  "ch1.model.title": "One PCI bus over 10 µs",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag the number of devices, the bandwidth each one wants, and the length of each transfer (in data phases). The top bar shows how the shared bus spends 10 µs: red is the fixed overhead of each transfer, and anything that does not fit is marked. Below it, one bar per device, as if each had a wire of its own.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "How one PCI bus spends 10 µs, and a comparison with each device on its own wire",
