  "ch1.read.p2p": "Each device with its own wire",
  "ch1.read.wait": "Longest wait for its turn",
  "ch1.cv.shared": "Shared bus: who uses the 10 µs",
  "ch1.cv.p2p": "Point-to-point: one wire each",
  "ch1.cv.x": "Time → 10 µs",
  "ch1.cv.missed": "{n} of {all} transfers do not fit in these 10 µs",
  "ch1.cv.allin": "All {n} transfers fit",
  "ch1.say.one": "With one device nobody competes with it: each transfer is {D} phases, and it gets <em>{each}</em>, which is all of the line’s rate after overhead.",
  "ch1.say.fits": "{n} devices each want {d}. The bus is busy for {u}, so everyone gets what they want. But before its turn comes, each one may wait as long as <em>{wait}</em>.",
  "ch1.say.full": "{n} devices each want {d}, which adds up to more than the {eff} left after overhead, so each gets only <em>{each}</em>. With each device on its own identical wire, each would get {p2p}, {tot} in all.",
  "ch1.p4": "The default is 6 devices at 30 MB/s each, with 16 phases per transfer. Overhead eats a third, so the bus can deliver only <strong>{eff16} MB/s</strong>, and each device gets <strong>{s1each} MB/s</strong>, less than half of what it wants. With a wire per device, each would get {s1p2p} MB/s, {s1tot} MB/s in all. Stretch each transfer to 64 phases and the overhead is spread thin: each device gets {s1bEach} MB/s, but the longest wait rises from {s1wait} µs to {s1bWait} µs. <strong>Long transfers trade for bandwidth, short ones for response time</strong>; a shared bus cannot give both at once. With one phase per transfer, the bus is left with only {eff1} MB/s.",
  "ch1.p5": "This is the basic problem of the <strong>shared bus</strong>: total bandwidth is fixed, so the more devices attached, the less each one gets. Everything that came after was an attempt to give each device its own wire, a <strong>point-to-point link</strong>. The price is more wires, and they need a hub that can take many of them at once (below, it is called the root, the northbridge or the chipset).",
  "ch1.limit": "<strong>What the model leaves out:</strong> it assumes fair round-robin arbitration with a fixed overhead of {initLat} clocks, as in the spec’s Table 3-5 example. Real devices wait different amounts of time, and the arbiter can also give some devices priority. The “point-to-point” column is only an imagined comparison that gives each device the same wires; it is not a real interface. The real point-to-point interface is the subject of “Why PCIe Keeps Doubling”.",

  "dg1.aria": "Structure diagram: on the left, every device hangs off one shared set of wires; on the right, each device has a wire of its own",
  "dg1.cap": "Shared bus and point-to-point. Schematic, not to scale.",
