  "ch2.title": "Why parallel buses can’t go faster",
  "ch2.law": "The more devices on a wire and the longer it runs, the longer a signal takes to make its round trip. At a fast clock, that trip no longer fits in one cycle.",
  "ch2.p1": "Flick one end of a long rope and the wave runs along it, then bounces back from the loose end. Long traces on a circuit board behave the same way: a signal travels at a finite speed, and at an unloaded end it <strong>reflects</strong>. PCI puts this to use in what it calls <strong>reflected-wave switching</strong>: the driver pushes the voltage only halfway, the wave runs to the open end and reflects back, and the reflection adds to the first half to reach a full level. The spec itself says this propagation takes at most {tp33} ns, a third of one 33 MHz cycle.",
  "ch2.p2": "Every device attached adds a small capacitance across the line. The spec allows at most {cin} pF per pin, card traces up to {stub} inches, and slots {slot} inches apart. More capacitance lowers the line’s <strong>characteristic impedance</strong> and slows the wave. This is the bind of a parallel bus: the more devices you attach, the slower the signal makes its round trips, and the faster the clock, the less time there is for them. (Skew between the wires is the other enemy of parallel buses. <a href=\"../how-sata-works/#ch2\">Chapter 2 of “Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”</a> works through that bill, so it is not repeated here.)",
  "ch2.p3": "The PCI spec splits each clock cycle into four parts: driver output Tval, propagation along the wire Tprop, clock skew Tskew and receiver setup time Tsu. The four must fit inside the cycle. At 33 MHz the cycle is 30 ns, leaving the wire <strong>{tp33} ns</strong>. Parts at 66 MHz are faster, but the cycle is only 15 ns, leaving the wire just <strong>{tp66} ns</strong>. The model below sets this timing budget against a wire loaded with devices.",
  "ch2.model.title": "How fast a bus can run with this many devices",
  "ch2.model.how": "Choose a part grade, then drag the clock, the number of loads and the capacitance of each load. The red line is the time the spec leaves for propagation along the wire; the gold dashed line is the round-trip time this wire needs. Where the two cross is the fastest clock the wire can run.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Propagation time budget against clock speed, and the round-trip time a loaded wire needs",
