  "ch2.cv.fmax": "Top speed about {f} MHz",
  "ch2.say.ok": "{n} loads at {f} MHz: one round trip takes {need}, and the spec leaves {T}, so it fits. This wire can run up to about <em>{fmax} MHz</em>, and at this clock it can carry at most {nmax} loads.",
  "ch2.say.ok2": "Each load’s capacitance is so large that one reflection only reaches {step}, short of 50%, so the wire needs {k} round trips. Even so, {need} still fits in {T}. Top speed about <em>{fmax} MHz</em>.",
  "ch2.say.late": "{n} loads at {f} MHz: one round trip takes {need}, but the spec leaves only {T}, so it <em>does not fit</em>. Either drop to about {fmax} MHz, or cut the loads to {nmax}.",
  "ch2.say.late2": "One reflection only reaches {step}, so the wire needs {k} round trips, {need} in all, more than the {T} left for it. It <em>does not fit</em>. The top speed is about {fmax} MHz, and at this clock at most {nmax} loads.",
  "ch2.say.nobudget": "At this clock, the part’s own Tval, Tsu and skew already use up the whole cycle, leaving the wire not one nanosecond: <em>no number of loads works</em>.",
  "ch2.p4": "By default each load counts as {cload} pF (the pin’s {cin} pF plus {stub} inches of card trace). Loading slows the wire to <strong>{tps} ps per inch</strong> and drops its impedance to about <strong>{zl} Ω</strong>. The weakest driver the spec allows acts like a {rs} Ω source resistance: its first step is only {v1}, and after one reflection it reaches {v1x2}, just past the 50% threshold, so one round trip is enough. Counting propagation alone, 33 MHz can take at most <strong>{n33}</strong> loads and 66 MHz only <strong>{n66}</strong>: double the clock and the devices you can attach halve. Raise each load to 20 pF and one reflection reaches only {v1b}, so an extra round trip is needed, and at 33 MHz only {n33b} loads fit.",
  "ch2.p5": "Later, PCI-X pushed the clock higher, at the cost of fewer slots per bus segment, until a segment held only one slot. At that point “shared” exists in name only. If each segment carries just one device, why not give each device its own serial point-to-point link, with the clock embedded in the data and no shared wire to reflect in? That path is PCIe. For how it doubles from one generation to the next, see “Why PCIe Keeps Doubling”.",
  "ch2.limit": "<strong>What the model leaves out:</strong> it counts only propagation and reflection, not edge rise time, ringing or the parasitics of each connector, so its numbers are upper bounds. The model says 33 MHz can take {n33} loads, but the spec’s driver curves were designed for a board with 6 slots plus 2, or 2 plus 4. The line speed of {tpd} ps per inch and the impedance of {z0} Ω are mid-points I took within the spec’s allowed ranges (150–190 ps per inch, 60–100 Ω). At 190 ps per inch the 33 MHz limit is {n33t190} loads; at 100 Ω it is {n33z100}. The driver sits at one end of the wire. Placed in the middle, it pushes both ways at once, and its first step is smaller.",

  "dg2.aria": "Structure diagram: a wire with several loads, and the voltage steps over time at the near and far ends",
  "dg2.cap": "Reflected-wave switching: the driver pushes only halfway, the open end reflects the wave, and the sum reaches the full level. The step heights on the right come from this chapter’s default settings; the time axis is schematic.",
  "dg2.a.title": "A wire with loads",
