  "ch3.read.gpu": "Graphics card actually gets",
  "ch3.read.sb": "Southbridge devices actually get",
  "ch3.read.mem": "Memory controller busy at",
  "ch3.bar.fsb": "Front-side bus",
  "ch3.bar.mem": "Memory (northbridge)",
  "ch3.bar.agp": "AGP",
  "ch3.bar.hi": "Hub Interface",
  "ch3.bar.csa": "CSA network port",
  "ch3.bar.fmt": "{u} / {cap}",
  "ch3.say.full": "The CPU wants {want} and gets <em>{cpu}</em>. The memory controller delivers {mem} in all, and it is not yet full.",
  "ch3.say.short": "The CPU wants {want} but gets only <em>{cpu}</em>. The front-side bus is wide enough; what is full is the memory controller in the northbridge, with {cap} to share among everyone.",
  "ch3.say.nocpu": "The CPU is not reading memory right now, so the memory controller carries only device traffic, {mem} in all.",
  "ch3.p4": "By default the CPU wants all {fsbG} GB/s, the graphics card wants {agpG}, and the southbridge and network card want a little more. The CPU actually gets only <strong>{nbCpu} GB/s</strong>; alone it could take {nbCpuAlone}. The front-side bus is not full. The memory controller in the northbridge is. The southbridge side is narrower still: two SATA disks at 150 MB/s each add up to {sata2} MB/s, already more than the southbridge’s {hiM} MB/s uplink, so in the model the southbridge devices get at most {nbSb} MB/s, however much they ask for.",
  "ch3.p5": "Most northbridge-era links are already point-to-point (AGP, Hub Interface and CSA are all one-to-one), but all the traffic still has to squeeze into one meeting point. The pattern did not disappear; the meeting point kept moving. In the next chapter it moved into the CPU, and in chapter 5 it became the chipset’s uplink.",
  "ch3.limit": "<strong>What the model leaves out:</strong> link capacities are the datasheet peaks. Real usable bandwidth is lower, since DDR bank switching and front-side bus turnaround both take time, and the front-side bus is itself a shared set of parallel wires. The model shares bandwidth only and ignores latency: every CPU memory access must cross the front-side bus and then the northbridge, a journey the next chapter takes apart. Here, the AGP link and the CSA port carry only traffic flowing to memory.",

  "dg3.aria": "Structure diagram of the Intel 875P platform. The CPU connects over the front-side bus to the northbridge, which connects to two memory channels, the AGP graphics card, a gigabit network port and the southbridge. The southbridge leads to SATA, USB, PCI and BIOS flash",
  "dg3.cap": "Intel 875P chipset (datasheet 252525-002, February 2004, Figure 1). Link bandwidths are the datasheet peaks; positions are schematic.",
