  "ch4.title": "Moving the memory controller into the CPU",
  "ch4.law": "Once the memory controller moved into the CPU, each CPU had its own memory. Put several CPUs in one machine and memory acquires near and far parts, so a program’s speed depends on where its data sits.",
  "ch4.p1": "The northbridge’s problem was that every CPU memory read took a detour: out of the CPU, across the front-side bus, into the northbridge, then on to the memory. In the 2000s, AMD and Intel in turn moved the <strong>memory controller onto the CPU</strong>. CPUs, and CPUs and other chips, began to talk over point-to-point serial links: AMD’s HyperTransport and later Infinity Fabric, Intel’s QPI and later UPI. The rest of the northbridge gradually moved into the CPU too.",
  "ch4.p2": "A server often has two CPUs, each in its own socket with its own memory, joined by a socket-to-socket link. If CPU 0 wants memory attached to CPU 1, it must ask CPU 1 to fetch it. A layout where all memory can be reached, but at different distances, is called <strong>NUMA</strong> (non-uniform memory access). AMD’s first-generation EPYC, in 2017, went further: one socket held four dies, each with two memory channels. The figures AMD gave at ISSCC 2018 were about <strong>{zLoc} ns</strong> to read memory on the same die, about <strong>{zp} ns</strong> to read a different die in the same socket, and about <strong>{zr} ns</strong> to read the other socket.",
  "ch4.p3": "How slow the average is depends on where the data sits: how much is local, how much is on another die in the same socket, and how much is across sockets. High latency also cuts bandwidth. A core can have only so many memory requests in flight at once, and under <a href=\"../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch4\">Little’s law</a>, bandwidth = requests in flight × 64 bytes ÷ latency.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Where the data sits decides how long a memory read takes",
  "ch4.model.how": "Drag the share of accesses that land on another die in the same socket and on the other socket; the rest land on the local die. Or use the three buttons below. The number of requests in flight is my own setting: drag it to see how bandwidth follows.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Latency and share of local, same-socket and cross-socket memory accesses, and the average latency",
  "ch4.btn.local": "All local",
  "ch4.btn.il": "Spread evenly over both sockets",
  "ch4.btn.far": "All on the other socket",
  "ch4.ctl.pkg": "On another die in the same socket",
  "ch4.ctl.remote": "On the other socket",
  "ch4.ctl.mlp": "Requests in flight per core (my setting)",
  "ch4.read.lat": "Average latency",
  "ch4.read.x": "times the all-local figure",
  "ch4.read.bw": "Bandwidth per core (Little’s law)",
  "ch4.read.local": "On the local die",
  "ch4.bar.local": "Local die",
  "ch4.bar.pkg": "Other die, same socket",
  "ch4.bar.remote": "Other socket",
  "ch4.bar.avg": "Average",
  "ch4.bar.fmt": "{lat} · share {s}",
  "ch4.say.local": "All the data sits in memory on the local die: each access takes <em>{lat}</em>, and each core can pull {bw}.",
  "ch4.say.mix": "{local} of accesses land on the local die, so the average is <em>{lat}</em>, {x} times the all-local figure. With {m} requests in flight per core, a core can pull only {bw}.",
  "ch4.say.far": "{remote} of accesses cross sockets. The average is <em>{lat}</em>, {x} times the all-local figure, and per-core bandwidth drops to {bw}.",
  "ch4.p4": "The default has the operating system spread memory evenly over the eight dies in the two sockets. Only one-eighth lands on the local die, {ilPkg} on another die in the same socket and {ilRemote} on the other socket. The average latency is <strong>{ilLat} ns</strong>, <strong>{ilX} times</strong> the all-local figure, and with 10 requests in flight per core, bandwidth falls from {bwLocal} GB/s to {bwIl} GB/s. That is why databases and virtual machines try to pin each thread to the node that holds its memory. Linux’s numactl and first-touch allocation both exist for this.",
  "ch4.p5": "Today’s two-socket servers keep the same structure, only with bigger numbers. Intel’s 2025 Xeon 6767P gives each socket {xch} channels of DDR5-6400, a peak of {sockBW} GB/s per socket. Two sockets are joined by {upiLinks} UPI links at {upiGT} GT/s each. However fast the socket-to-socket link, it cannot match a local memory controller, so NUMA distance remains. For what happens when many cores fight over the same memory channels, see <a href=\"../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch5\">chapter 5 of “Why Compute Waits for Memory”</a>.",
  "ch4.limit": "<strong>What the model leaves out:</strong> the three latencies are AMD’s idle figures (2.4 GHz cores, DDR4-2667). Under load the cross-socket path queues first, and the gap grows. Cross-socket access also needs a cache-coherence check, which is not modelled separately. The 10 requests in flight per core are my own choice. Bandwidth is proportional to them, so halving them halves the bandwidth: in the all-local case, half of {bwLocal} GB/s. Today’s CPUs have different numbers, but the same structure.",

  "dg4.aria": "Structure diagram: two CPUs, each in its own socket with four dies. Dies inside a socket are linked, the two sockets share one link, and each socket has its own memory",
  "dg4.cap": "Two AMD EPYC 7001 processors: four dies per socket, each die with two memory channels. Latencies come from AMD’s ISSCC 2018 presentation; the layout is schematic.",
  "dg4.link": "Socket-to-socket link",
  "dg4.s0": "Socket 0",
  "dg4.s1": "Socket 1",
  "dg4.die": "Die",
