  "dg4.lat1": "Local die {zLoc} ns · same-socket die {zp} ns",
  "dg4.lat2": "Other socket {zr} ns",

  "ch5.title": "Today’s motherboard",
  "ch5.law": "On today’s motherboard, the graphics card and the fastest SSDs connect directly to the CPU. The other devices hang off the chipset, which shares one uplink to the CPU.",
  "ch5.p1": "With the northbridge now fully inside the CPU, the chip that remains on the board is the old southbridge, now called the <strong>chipset</strong> (Intel calls it the PCH). Take Intel’s Core Ultra 200S and Z890 chipset from late 2024 (figures from Intel’s product pages and chipset overview). The CPU itself provides {cpuGen5} PCIe 5.0 lanes, which can be split into one ×16 plus one ×4 (or two ×8 plus one ×4, or one ×8 plus three ×4), another 4 PCIe 4.0 lanes, and two DDR5 memory channels. Between the CPU and the chipset runs one <strong>DMI 4.0 ×{dmiLanes}</strong> link.",
  "ch5.p2": "Below the chipset, up to {pchLanes} PCIe 4.0 lanes branch out, along with up to {sataPorts} SATA ports, more than ten USB ports, networking, Wi-Fi and a small interface called eSPI (which connects to the BIOS flash and the board’s management chip). These ports do not each have their own wires. The chipset has only {hsio} high-speed lanes in all, shared among PCIe, USB 3 and SATA, so using one takes away from another. That is why motherboard manuals often say that once a certain M.2 drive is installed, two particular SATA ports stop working.",
  "ch5.p3": "However many devices sit under the chipset, anything that deals with the CPU and memory goes over that one uplink. DMI 4.0 runs 16 GT/s per lane with PCIe 4.0 encoding, so ×{dmiLanes} gives about <strong>{dmi} GB/s</strong> in each direction. It is full duplex: data read from devices toward memory and data written to devices travel on separate paths. On the AMD side, the Ryzen 9000 has {amdTotal} PCIe lanes in all, {amdUsable} of them usable, and the other 4 go to the chipset. At PCIe 4.0 ×4, that is about <strong>{amdLink} GB/s</strong> in each direction. This is the sharing from chapter 1, only moved from one set of wires to one uplink.",
  "ch5.model.title": "Devices under the chipset, all busy at once",
  "ch5.model.how": "Choose a platform, then have the two M.2 SSDs under the chipset read or write. Tick the USB portable drive and the two SATA SSDs, and tick an SSD on the CPU for comparison. The top two rows are the uplink and downlink. Below them, one row per device: grey is what it wants, colour is what it actually gets.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Load on the chipset’s uplink and downlink, and what each device wants and actually gets",
  "ch5.leg.rate": "Actually gets",
  "ch5.leg.want": "Wants",
