  "ch5.dev.none": "No device is busy",
  "ch5.say.idle": "Every device under the chipset is idle, so the uplink carries no load.",
  "ch5.say.cpuonly": "Only the SSD attached to the CPU is reading. It uses the CPU’s own lanes and <em>takes nothing from the chipset uplink</em>.",
  "ch5.say.fits": "Uplink {up}, downlink {dn}, both under {U}: every device gets what it wants.",
  "ch5.say.duplex": "Uplink {up}, downlink {dn}: reading and writing at once, each direction on its own lanes, <em>both fit</em>. On a full-duplex link, a disk-to-disk copy does not compete with itself.",
  "ch5.say.over": "Devices under the chipset want {over} times the link’s capacity, and {U} has to be shared. The worst-off device gets only <em>{slow}</em> of what it wants.",
  "ch5.p4": "The default has two chipset-attached SSDs reading at once (each wants {nvme} GB/s, the sequential read figure on a PCIe 4.0 SSD datasheet), plus a USB portable drive ({usb20} GB/s). Together they want {bdWant} GB/s upstream, a little more than DMI’s {dmi} GB/s, so the USB drive gets what it asks for and each SSD gets <strong>{bdSsd} GB/s</strong>. Move to AMD’s ×4 link and the same mix leaves each SSD only <strong>{bdaSsd} GB/s</strong>. But if you copy from SSD 1 to SSD 2, reading and writing use the two directions separately, and both platforms can run the full {copyI} GB/s. The SSD attached to the CPU never touches this link.",
  "ch5.p5": "So “is this M.2 slot wired to the CPU or to the chipset?” is worth asking; the motherboard manual’s block diagram shows it. A single drive on its own shows no difference. The difference appears only when several devices are busy at once. How much a ×16 slot gains over ×8 is a question about PCIe itself: see “Why PCIe Keeps Doubling”.",
  "ch5.limit": "<strong>What the model leaves out:</strong> links are counted at their peak rate in each direction, with protocol overhead ignored. SSDs are assumed to hold their datasheet sequential speeds, while real use is usually short bursts. Switching inside the chipset and the USB controller’s own limit are not modelled. The AMD link is counted as PCIe 4.0 ×4; that generation figure comes from review material, not from AMD’s product pages, which give only {amdTotal} / {amdUsable} lanes. On X870E boards two chipsets are actually chained, with the second routed through the first. The model does not draw that.",

  "dg5.aria": "Structure diagram of a 2024 desktop. The CPU connects directly to memory, the graphics slot and two M.2 slots, and to the Z890 chipset over DMI 4.0 ×8. The chipset leads to M.2, USB, SATA, network, Wi-Fi and eSPI",
  "dg5.cap": "Intel Core Ultra 200S with Z890 (Intel product pages and 800-series chipset overview, consulted November 2026). Which slot connects where is decided by the motherboard maker; positions are schematic.",
  "dg5.mem": "DDR5 ×2 channels",
